panels.description:
Three failure layers in one panel, read top-down:

* `up{job=~"$job"}` — 0 means Prometheus could not reach the target at all: nothing was scraped, and every other panel on this dashboard is showing history;
* `min(llm_monitor_scrape_ok)` — 0 means the monitor is up but *its* scrape of the model server failed (no /metrics route, timeout, 5xx). Different incident, different runbook section, and precisely the distinction the  bespoke 'is it down?' tile could not make;
* `increase(llm_monitor_scrape_success_total[...])` — successful passthrough scrapes in the window, the canonical 'is the monitor actually scraping' signal (ADR 0001 C2);
* `time() - timestamp(up{...})` — how stale the newest sample is, which is how a target that answers but has stopped advancing (a proxy replaying a buffered response) becomes visible.

The `llm_monitor_*` series are deliberately NOT clamped by $job/$instance: 'is the telemetry pipeline itself alive' has to stay answerable while the variables are narrowed to one server, and the `upstream` label already separates several monitors (one proxy can front several servers via `/metrics?url=`).

PromQL sent by this panel:

```promql
A: up{job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}
A: min(llm_monitor_scrape_ok)
A: sum(increase(llm_monitor_scrape_success_total[$__rate_interval]))
A: max(time() - timestamp(up{job=~"$job",instance=~"$instance"}))
```
